# The usual worry about this passage is that "miracle" means a violation of the laws of nature in S1 but something never observed in S3 and S5.
# On the statements as given, P1, P2, C1 and C all read "miracle" as a violation of the laws of nature. S3 and S5 offer "never observed" as a test for spotting one, not a second meaning of the word.
# "Common course of nature" could mean happening by natural law or having been observed. Both P2 and A1 use the same gloss ("has been observed"), so it does not shift between them.
# A1 moves from never observed to a uniform experience against. Whether that experience must also count the reported miracle itself is a question of whether the argument is sound, not a change in what either claim means.
